WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.300
Ho!

9
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:27.680
Ho, ho, ho.

10
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.800
Are you ready for some Christmas wishes?

11
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.580
Boy, am I.

12
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.480
This is Season 8, Episode 10.

13
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.680
It was written by Mindy Kaling.

14
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.480
This was her third Christmas episode, Angela.

15
00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.160
I did not realize she was the go-to Christmas writer until we started this rewatch.

16
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.820
Apparently so.

17
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.200
She also wrote Diwali.

18
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:54.020
She's like our, I guess, if it's a holiday, give it to Mindy.

19
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:54.240
Yeah.

20
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.260
This episode was also directed by Ed HelMs.

21
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.140
This was his directorial debut.

22
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.560
So I would do this thing when we had a cast member who directed for the first time.

23
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.580
I would get everyone to sign the script and give it to them as a gift.

24
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:08.360
Aw.

25
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.860
Yeah, and I gave it to Ed, and he was like, Ange, I love this.

26
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.040
Well, I was not in this episode.

27
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.080
I was at home with my new baby, so I did not get to participate in any of the fun stuff that happened.

28
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.060
And oh my gosh, this episode has a lot.

29
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:23.780
Here's your summary.

30
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:32.240
Determined to make this the best office Christmas party ever, Andy promises to make each employee's holiday wish come true.

31
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.660
Meanwhile, Dwight and Jim are pranking one another, and someone has complained.

32
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.060
Mm-hmm.

33
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.140
Someone in their desk clump.

34
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.320
Yes.

35
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.700
And Andy gives them an ultimatum.

36
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.540
Yes, he does.

37
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.680
He says, the two of you need to stop pranking each other, or I'm going to give your Christmas bonuses to the other person.

38
00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:23.640
Mm-hmm.

39
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:30.860
Well, listen, everyone, we are going to kick off this episode of Office Ladies with an interview with the one and only Amina Kaplan.

40
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.540
You know, she appeared as Val in 14 episodes of The Office, including Christmas Wishes.

41
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:44.420
Amina attended Tisch School of the Arts in New York, and she has won theater awards for choreography, and she can play the druMs.

42
00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.980
Can she play the drums?

43
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.640
She's like a professional drummer and now a director.

44
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.940
We can't wait for you to hear this interview.

45
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.540
We love her.

46
00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:53.960
Yes.

47
00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.180
So without further ado, here you go.

48
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.100
Woo-hoo.

49
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:02.940
Hey, everybody.

50
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:03.580
What's up?

51
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:04.460
How y'all doing?

52
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:05.880
Oh, we're good.

53
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.940
So good to see you.

54
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.280
It's good to see you, too.

55
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.100
It's been a while.

56
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.600
It's been a hot minute, in fact.

57
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:12.320
It's been a long time.

58
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:12.980
It sure has.

59
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.360
And we're Zooming with you today.

60
00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:15.240
You're in New Orleans?

61
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.080
I am.

62
00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.380
This is my first time in New Orleans.

63
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.660
It's awesome down here.

64
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:20.280
It's been a special place to me.

65
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.500
I was born in Louisiana.

66
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.120
What?

67
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:28.420
Yeah, I was born in Lafayette, and I did two movies in New Orleans, and my family would come and visit.

68
00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.480
And so I'm going to email you a few spots that you've got to check out.

69
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.320
I would love that, because I have the rest of my afternoon, like after 4 o'clock, I'm going to be free.

70
00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:42.520
So that's all I'm going to do is just go around to spots in New Orleans, because we're working hard here.

71
00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.520
So we have this full schedule, although I took this hour out to hang out with you guys.

72
00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.040
Thank you.

73
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.440
Oh, we love that.

74
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.060
It's my pleasure.

75
00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:49.760
Thank you so much.

76
00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.300
Well, we'll jump right in then.

77
00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:58.240
We're going to ask you the question that we ask every guest to kick things off, which is, how did you get your job on The Office?

78
00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:58.940
Aha.

79
00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.140
Awesome casting director, Alison Jones, cast me.

80
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.500
I was literally just an actor in L.A., just doing my thing.

81
00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.900
And it literally just came through as a regular audition.

82
00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:11.680
There it was.

83
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.280
Just like, boom, there's a character on a TV show.

84
00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.520
You're right for it.

85
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.080
You're going to go in for that job.

86
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:24.880
And it turned out to be serendipitous and special in that I was acquainted with Craig Robinson before I went into my audition.

87
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.620
I did not know he was going to be at the callback.

88
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.000
I didn't actually see him.

89
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.220
He saw me.

90
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.440
If you ask Craig, Craig would be like, you don't even know, like, how much I got you on that show.

91
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.300
So I'm like, all right, Craig, whatever I owe it to you.

92
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.580
So I give him the credit.

93
00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.300
Like, I think Craig really was like, oh, snap, that's Amina.

94
00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:41.980
I know Amina.

95
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.000
And, you know, we're both musicians.

96
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.840
And so we kind of knew each other from the music scene.

97
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.860
Yeah, you play drums, right?

98
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:49.600
That's right.

99
00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:49.860
Yeah.

100
00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:50.560
I'm a drummer.

101
00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.840
I honestly feel like that's kind of the only real thing that I am.

102
00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.380
I'm a hack with everything else.

103
00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:01.320
Because looking back at it at this age, I'm like, yeah, drumming, that's the thing that I did when I was nine years old.

104
00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.720
I just was beating on stuff.

105
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:07.300
I was like hitting things and making rhythms and making beats on desktops and stuff.

106
00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:12.200
And when I was 12, a guy taught me how to play Bullet to Blue Sky by U2 on the drum set.

107
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.020
And I was like, OK.

108
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.980
And I was terrible for years and years and years because I had no training.

109
00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.540
And my family was like, what do you think you're doing playing drums?

110
00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:20.960
And I was like, I'm doing this.

111
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.860
But, yeah, I'm a drummer and have been.

112
00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.620
This will be a long time.

113
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:28.380
I'm like, this year will be 40 years of playing once I, like later this year.

114
00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.400
And so, yeah, it is the only thing that I really am.

115
00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:31.740
Yeah.

116
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:32.980
And Craig's a musician.

117
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.820
And we were in a band full of actors.

118
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.140
So if anybody got a gig, nobody was worried.

119
00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.220
It was like, OK, whatever.

120
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.000
I booked the thing.

121
00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:39.740
OK, whatever.

122
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.140
And Craig was friends with the guys in the band.

123
00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.240
And they used to hang out.

124
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.840
And being the girl in the band, I never really hung out with them.

125
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.120
But we were around the same scene.

126
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:48.880
Right, right.

127
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:49.180
Yeah.

128
00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:53.120
Oh, in my heart right now, if there ever is an office reunion,

129
00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.180
Val's got to play the druMs.

130
00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.240
Val's got to boot Kevin out of the band.

131
00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:58.580
I know.

132
00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.800
Was it killing you when you had to sit and listen to them play?

133
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.400
And you're like, I could be the drummer in Kevin in the Zits.

134
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.260
I'm not even going to mince words.

135
00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.360
The answer is yes.

136
00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.160
I was standing there.

137
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.440
And I was like, you've got to be kidding me right now.

138
00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.060
They are playing drums like two feet in front of me.

139
00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.060
This is crazy.

140
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:18.960
Like, why am I standing here?

141
00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.860
And why is Val not on the drums?

142
00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.440
Why is she not the secret killer?

143
00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.060
And, you know, it's just one of those things.

144
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.020
I was the new kid.

145
00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.320
It was Brian's moment.

146
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.220
And it was awesome.

147
00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.940
We had the drummers at the wrong band.

148
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:29.820
It was just heaven.

149
00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.440
That day was that episode was just heaven for me.

150
00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.260
And totally frustrating that I didn't speak up about being a drummer.

151
00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.080
I kind of kept those worlds separate.

152
00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.040
Because when I got to L.A., it was weird.

153
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.700
Like, coming from New York, where you could be a triple threat, as they called it or whatever.

154
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:45.000
Right.

155
00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:46.860
Coming to L.A., it was like, well, what are you?

156
00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:47.600
Are you an actor?

157
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:48.320
Are you a drummer?

158
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:49.040
Are you a dancer?

159
00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.160
So, I never told anybody that I did the other thing.

160
00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.320
I did the other thing to keep from waiting tables, you know?

161
00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:55.640
Yeah.

162
00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.140
And so, but I never told anybody that I played druMs.

163
00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.200
If you knew I was a drummer, you knew.

164
00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:04.280
So, I didn't come into my acting auditions like, I'm a drummer.

165
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.780
You know, I'm trying to book a job as like a young mom or something.

166
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:08.280
Yeah.

167
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.400
So, I didn't come waving my rock and roll flag, so.

168
00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:16.980
It is so interesting because during that time, we were all auditioning around the same time back then.

169
00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:19.620
And it really was kind of like, stay in your lane.

170
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.020
You know, you're an actor.

171
00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.780
But I feel like now people really share.

172
00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.480
I think social media has helped that, that you can share all the things you do.

173
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.000
And people learn, oh, you're this, this, and this.

174
00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:30.720
And that's great.

175
00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:31.400
Yeah.

176
00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:32.260
You know what?

177
00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.500
I'm going to say we did that.

178
00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.740
We pioneered that because I was doing it before social media.

179
00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:37.880
I'm from the 90s.

180
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.100
Everybody on this podcast, we're from the 90s.

181
00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.420
Let's get our credit, Gen X.

182
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:42.640
Let's get our credit.

183
00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.280
Because we were doing it in the cut.

184
00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.400
Like I'm saying, I just didn't tell anybody.

185
00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.200
Right, right.

186
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:53.380
And if people did find out, I actually got made fun of one time because somebody was like, oh, really?

187
00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.780
You think you're all like, oh, so you're an actor and you're a musician?

188
00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.380
You know, nobody was encouraging this little chocolate drop to get into this business.

189
00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:02.600
So I took precautions.

190
00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:03.720
I didn't brag.

191
00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.340
I wasn't around telling people that I played with Rihanna or that I had played with Macy

192
00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:14.240
Gray or I just was like in there sitting down just trying to survive, just trying to do

193
00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.280
a good job, you know.

194
00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.580
But I do think that now we know that you have to be a multi-hyphenate.

195
00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.760
We're artists, which means that we are absorbing things from everywhere.

196
00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.240
We're taking inspiration from everything.

197
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.240
And we are all multifaceted.

198
00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:30.980
And I'm glad it's being celebrated now.

199
00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:31.360
I'm glad.

200
00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.160
Well, you know, you brought something up that reminds me of what my best acting coach ever

201
00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.660
told me about making it in Hollywood or in an artistic career.

202
00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:47.860
And what that is, is that you need to cultivate a consistent body of work where every time

203
00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:52.640
you show up to do a job, you are consistent, you are good, you do your thing.

204
00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:55.500
And that is what is going to propel you forward.

205
00:08:55.500 --> 00:09:00.560
It's not meeting someone at a party or schmoozing someone in a meeting.

206
00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.020
Your work is what speaks for you.

207
00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.520
It sounds like that's what you're talking about.

208
00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:10.840
Every time in this world, it was just like people showing up, creating a consistent body

209
00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.540
of work and everybody just being their best artist self.

210
00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:15.620
We had to be.

211
00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.380
You know, Jenna, it was like it was one of those things like I was saying, like people weren't

212
00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.520
encouraging me to get into the arts.

213
00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:27.380
The arts called me and I recognize that now from my 40s that I literally was just doing

214
00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:27.700
it.

215
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:28.840
I was one of those kids.

216
00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.260
So that's what we did.

217
00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:32.260
I knew that I would have to make art.

218
00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:33.360
I knew it, you know.

219
00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.400
And when I met you guys, when we were doing the office, I was already in film school.

220
00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:42.100
I had already made this decision that I was going to go make less money directing live

221
00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:45.700
entertainment because that's where my heart was, was in live entertainment and just really

222
00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:48.520
viscerally connecting to the audiences, you know.

223
00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:52.880
And I was trained from a young age that you make art, you make art, you make art.

224
00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.380
And so that was what we did.

225
00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.220
And so those were the people that I gravitated to.

226
00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.400
And so those like-minded people, yeah, we connected.

227
00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.900
And I think there were a lot of those people on the office, like on the show.

228
00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:11.300
The majority of our cast had been just for years trying to find their artistic avenue.

229
00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:15.020
You know, for me, I was doing improv for like eight years.

230
00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:19.100
I was on stage every chance I got and everyone was doing things like that.

231
00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.660
And there weren't any overnight successes.

232
00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:26.240
I mean, we had some young writers, you know, but they had been doing their thing in college.

233
00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:33.340
But when I look at our cast, we had all been there for years just doing our art and waiting

234
00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:34.880
for our moment and being ready.

235
00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:35.660
That's right.

236
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:36.860
I'm in the same bucket.

237
00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.000
Like people ask me, you know, what did you think of the office or whatever?

238
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.540
That's one of the main things that I say is that Alison Jones, that casting director,

239
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.860
she was looking in the cut for us.

240
00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.880
All those performers like us who were the lifers, who maybe we didn't look a certain way

241
00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.660
or whatever, like whatever those boxes are, we didn't check them.

242
00:10:54.860 --> 00:11:00.820
We checked the box of this is what I must do because this is who I am and I'm going to

243
00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:05.600
do it and I'm going to get a part because I have to, because I am a working actor.

244
00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.800
You know, we're just going to be in that category.

245
00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.660
Maybe we will get uber famous.

246
00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:10.480
Maybe we won't.

247
00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:13.440
But I'm going to tell you right now, you're going to know because we're in the caSt.

248
00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.700
And Alison was looking for us.

249
00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:20.520
And it took us years because, again, we weren't doing it the same way.

250
00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.600
We weren't going to be the splash in the pan.

251
00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:24.560
And I'm forever grateful to that.

252
00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.720
And I walked in there and I felt that energy.

253
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:27.880
I could feel you guys.

254
00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:29.000
I was like, oh, OK.

255
00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:35.100
Now, granted, y'all were seven years ahead of me in terms of down the line, you know.

256
00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:37.440
But I was like, these people are real up in here.

257
00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:38.760
These are the actors right here.

258
00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:40.260
Alison, she's done this.

259
00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:41.920
She's curated this caSt.

260
00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:43.240
And I'm grateful.

261
00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:46.380
I'm grateful to that because it took me a long time.

262
00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.200
Like I said, I'm alternative.

263
00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:53.320
You know, I look a certain way that maybe betrays the way I speak and my background or whatever

264
00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:53.740
it is.

265
00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:59.680
And it took Hollywood and the world, frankly, a long time to try and figure out what I am,

266
00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.380
who I am and where I'm coming from.

267
00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.520
But Alison was like, I got you.

268
00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:04.840
She's so great.

269
00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:06.180
Yeah, I got you.

270
00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.900
In addition, did you know that this was going to be a multi-episode arc?

271
00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.500
Did you know that it was going to be a love story?

272
00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:18.640
So I knew about the potential, but they tested me out.

273
00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.820
Like they screen tested me in my first couple of episodes.

274
00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:26.640
But I had an inkling that it would go well if I did a good job.

275
00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.120
But I needed to do a good job in order for it to happen, you know.

276
00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:37.840
And I was happy to be in that spot because I felt so strongly that the writers were working

277
00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:43.060
with me, that they wanted me to succeed, that Paul and everybody wanted me to succeed.

278
00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:45.480
So it just felt in the pocket.

279
00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:46.840
Like I felt that support.

280
00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:49.640
I felt support from John Krasinski when I first got there.

281
00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.820
I just felt people going, come on in, Amina.

282
00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:54.140
You know, we want you to succeed.

283
00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:55.440
And so it was great.

284
00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:58.040
Well, you know, we're just watching Christmas Wishes.

285
00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:02.000
And that's when your character comes up to the bullpen, right?

286
00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:03.100
The popsicles.

287
00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:04.880
Comes and joins the popsicles.

288
00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:08.720
And I remember that was really the first time we all got to work with you.

289
00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:10.680
I know you had all these scenes in the warehouse.

290
00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:12.420
But now you're up in the bullpen.

291
00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.000
You're up in the Dunder Mifflin office.

292
00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.080
You looked so fantastic.

293
00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:17.760
Thank you.

294
00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:18.720
You looked so beautiful.

295
00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:20.860
You really do, Amina.

296
00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:25.320
And now, you know, I was disappointed that I did not get to be there in person for your

297
00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.580
big bullpen scenes.

298
00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.740
But I wasn't in Christmas Wishes.

